                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b6bd8f0c-b3b0-4a4e-81ae-b51ed6dd5654",
                        "entry_id": "dca0a688-dbfe-3e13-847f-8b766d7c654e",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "capreols"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7b30202c-ca08-3c79-bfed-ba560bb04c59",
                        "entry_id": "dca0a688-dbfe-3e13-847f-8b766d7c654e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A high leap."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1a12706a-167d-43f0-a6f8-0a5919987a35",
                                "sense_id": "7b30202c-ca08-3c79-bfed-ba560bb04c59",
                                "text": "Ixion[…]turn'd dancer, does nothing but cut capreols, fetch friskals, and leads lavaltoes"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "550e666a-fdba-4cd9-9b8d-396d76653788",
                                "sense_id": "7b30202c-ca08-3c79-bfed-ba560bb04c59",
                                "text": "Run through the field with frisks and kicks, In various capreols and tricks, Some ease poor thing, alass, to find;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "505aaf0a-fa21-45ad-9233-b7787c6a77db",
                                "sense_id": "7b30202c-ca08-3c79-bfed-ba560bb04c59",
                                "text": "A little Wall lay sculking about this Territory of the Dead, which we suppos'd was plac'd there as a Bulwark to their Ashes, but it prov'd but a feeble Fence against the Intrusion of the Lambs, who made frequent Capreols into this adjacent Dormitory."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d024f234-794d-402c-b8ef-8016d516f8f3",
                                "sense_id": "7b30202c-ca08-3c79-bfed-ba560bb04c59",
                                "text": "Ye know the capers our friends cut, their transnatural leaps exceed the capreols at Sadler's, or those of Del Phobo, or the dance of Merlin's giants: fools!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "86fd83ad-ec31-46cb-9cd1-16572a5744b5",
                                "sense_id": "7b30202c-ca08-3c79-bfed-ba560bb04c59",
                                "text": "Noblissimo's First Gentleman remarks 'Your Lordship rid to day beyond Perseus on his Pegasus' and Noblissimo, modestly deferring, 'No Monsieur, he went (if Poets speak truth) in higher Capreols than ever I shall make my Horse go'."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9ac2fc38-3943-380c-b4aa-eabbcdde76f0",
                        "entry_id": "dca0a688-dbfe-3e13-847f-8b766d7c654e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A billy goat or small roebuck."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "10f70777-5642-4dd5-8da5-d43148011c75",
                                "sense_id": "9ac2fc38-3943-380c-b4aa-eabbcdde76f0",
                                "text": "the wild boare and sow, red and fallow deere, roebuck, and capreol, hare, conie, and squirrels, & c. of which, see more in their proper places."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "04a13f48-e199-4bcb-8536-f774576237d7",
                                "sense_id": "9ac2fc38-3943-380c-b4aa-eabbcdde76f0",
                                "text": "It may be remarked, that Bellenden, in the translation of Boethius which he undertook (probably about the year 1536) at the request of King James V., while he omits the cervi, capreols, and even the lutroe, mentions bevers without the slightest hesitation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "31c4f2d0-21fe-4db4-9162-ee2f0c05b0f5",
                                "sense_id": "9ac2fc38-3943-380c-b4aa-eabbcdde76f0",
                                "text": "In the Latin of course the billy goat is caper from which we have the obsolete word capreol, meaning nothing more nor less than a plain billy goat. But if I had spoken of a capreol my untutored enemies would never have understood my drift."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9b2ed167-7b2a-3db6-a5c7-2cda5129d19c",
                        "entry_id": "dca0a688-dbfe-3e13-847f-8b766d7c654e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A tendril."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5a623083-cc0c-42f5-bde3-0145897219e4",
                                "sense_id": "9b2ed167-7b2a-3db6-a5c7-2cda5129d19c",
                                "text": "For the Gourd is full of braunches, and beareth great broad leaues, and by the helpe of tendrels, or capreols quickly claspeth, catcheth hold, and climbeth vp to a great height, and maketh a pleasant Arbor to sit vnder, and to defende a man from the heat of the pearching Sunne."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02b19530-a010-426b-bfa6-efb5e53c3377",
                                "sense_id": "9b2ed167-7b2a-3db6-a5c7-2cda5129d19c",
                                "text": "The white beares white berryes, and somtimes white leafes, emitting certaine branches or capreols out of the midst of its leafs, wherewith it so strictly complects the Trees, that it kills them by sucking their humour from them;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "74b19edc-beb9-4724-9e1a-2fd0d994fe02",
                                "sense_id": "9b2ed167-7b2a-3db6-a5c7-2cda5129d19c",
                                "text": "Vine, Vitis, the Capreols, Tendrels, and Claspers ( like those of the Hop, & c. ) whilst very young, have an agreeable Acid, which may be eaten alone, or with other Sallet ."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "aaab0cc7-c383-3056-8d0f-f1231ed4d0fa",
                        "entry_id": "dca0a688-dbfe-3e13-847f-8b766d7c654e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A side brace."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c25ef507-8eb2-4200-9686-3d5a189a22b5",
                                "sense_id": "aaab0cc7-c383-3056-8d0f-f1231ed4d0fa",
                                "text": "Over these other beams, wellwrought from timbers of 2 fee , are placed around; upon which the transtræ and capreols, ( the principal rafters and braces, ) being placed coincident with the zophorus, antæ , and walls of the pronaos, sustain one culmen (the horizontal piece of timber in the ridge of the roof) the whole length of the basilica, and another transversely from the middle over the pronaos of the temple;"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
